          "Once you strip away the high-brow terminology, Kubernetes is built around just five core building blocks:",
          "**1. Cluster:** The entire ecosystem. A collection of connected machines (servers/VMs) pooled together as one giant super-computer.",
          "**2. Node:** A single machine in the cluster. It can be a physical server in your closet (On-Premise) or an EC2 instance in the cloud. There are **Master Nodes** (which form the Control Plane brain) and **Worker Nodes** (the muscle that actually runs your apps).",
          "**3. Pod:** The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes. K8s doesn't run containers directly on nodes; it wraps one or more containers inside a **Pod**. Think of a Pod as a pea pod: usually, it holds a single container (like your Node.js app), gives it a unique IP address, and shares local storage.",
          "**4. Deployment & ReplicaSet:** This is where the magic happens. Instead of saying *'run this pod'*, you tell Kubernetes: *'I want 3 replicas of my web app running at all times.'* If Pod #2 crashes or someone accidentally pulls the plug on Node 1, the Deployment controller detects the discrepancy and **instantly spins up a replacement Pod on another healthy node (Self-Healing)**.",
          "**5. Service & Ingress:** Pods are ephemeral; they can die and be recreated with new internal IP addresses at any moment. A **Service** acts as a stable, permanent doorway with a fixed internal IP and built-in load balancer. An **Ingress** is the front door that connects your domain name (`https://myapp.com`) to the internal Service."
